+++ b/registry/allocations_mk2.toml
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# Single source of truth for every scarce resource a mod can claim - Analog Rytm MKII,
+# OS 1.73 (corp MK2 port, TRUTH D34-D38; `make DEVICE=mk2 ...`). The same rules as the
+# MK1 registry (registry/allocations.toml): tools/layout.py asserts that no two claims
+# overlap, that every claim sits inside its pool and that nothing touches the
+# never-write range; tools/build.py checks every `expect` against the stock MK2 MAIN OS
+# bytes before patching.
+#
+# Every address below is an MK2 address; mk1 = ... names the MK1 1.73 twin where one
+# exists. Each expect was re-read from the MK2 MAIN OS (sha256 28d9ef40...85d6) and
+# matches the corp MK2 RE lanes' (mk2-addrB sites.json, skeptic-confirmed).
+#
+# owner = the mod directory name, or "reserved:<reason>".
+
+[meta]
+target = "OS1.73"
+device = "Analog Rytm MKII"
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the image
+#
+# corp mk2-space lane and its skeptic (all static, on this image):
+#   __bss_start = 0x402ce000 (the startup clear starts there, 0x400007e6 'lea
+#   0x402ce000,%a2'), so nothing below it is .bss and the clear never touches a pool.
+#   The C++ static-constructor table (146 entries on MK2, 130 on MK1) ends at
+#   0x402cd89c; the USB string block starts at 0x402cd000 and its first byte (bLength)
+#   is written at run time, so `cave` must end exactly at 0x402cd000.
+#
+# THE MK2 TRAP (TRUTH D35): 0x402c8754..0x402cc91c is the embedded firmware of another
+# device, which an MK2-only updater sends at startup (re/symbols_mk2.toml [meta]). No
+# pool may touch it; layout.py refuses any claim, patch or detour that does, and
+# build.py and verify.py hold it to its stock hash in every build.
+#
+# Layout A (corp mk2-space, TRUTH D37): MK1's layout copied as is overflows MK2's cave2
+# (MK2's cave2 is 288 B smaller: 16 more constructors), so 0001's .cave2 code moves to
+# `cave` and its .cave3 constants to `cave2`. All run-time state (CHOP's 48 B at the end
+# of .chst, 0008's 128-B filter state .cutst) sits in `cave`, the twin of MK1's cave.
+
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- pools
+
+[[pool]]
+name   = "cave"
+start  = 0x402cc928
+end    = 0x402cd000          # exclusive; 1752 B
+status = "probably-verified"
+note   = """
+The MK2 twin of MK1's cave (and of the MKII project's cave1): padding at the end of
+the code, before the UTF-16 USB string block at 0x402cd000. What ends just before it is
+the embedded device firmware, whose exclusive bound 0x402cc91c is an immediate in its
+updater; the pool starts 12 bytes after it, as MK1's does after its bootstrap.
+
+Evidence (corp mk2-space + skeptic): 0x402cc91c..0x402cd001 is zeros; no 32-bit word at
+any offset in the image points into the pool; it is below __bss_start 0x402ce000, so
+nothing clears or reuses it at run time; the byte at 0x402cd000 is the USB block's
+first byte, written at run time, so the pool ends exactly there. MK1's twin carried
+run-time state in the round-4 image (founder's MK1 runs); the MKII peer's cave1 ran on
+MKII hardware (a claim in this repo's notes, not re-checked here).
+"""
+
+[[pool]]
+name   = "cave2"
+start  = 0x402cd8a0
+end    = 0x402ce000          # exclusive; 1888 B
+status = "probably-verified"
+note   = """
+The MK2 twin of MK1's cave2: padding between the end of the static-constructor table
+(146 entries, ending 0x402cd89c) and __bss_start 0x402ce000 (corp mk2-space + skeptic:
+zero run 0x402cd89c..0x402d3820, no word in the image points into the pool). Starts 4
+bytes in to keep claims aligned. No run-time state here (layout A): code and constants.
+"""
+
+[[pool]]
+name   = "cave3"
+start  = 0x40289ab8
+end    = 0x40289eb8          # exclusive; 1024 B
+status = "probably-verified"
+note   = """
+The MK2 twin of MK1's cave3 (1024 B of zeros between two float tables; the MKII peer
+ran 0003 here). Constants and code only, never run-time state (TRUTH D37), for MK1's
+reason and one more (corp mk2-space skeptic): stock reads that index with a signed SRAM
+byte and no lower bound (MK2 twins of MK1's 0x800066d8/0x800066da: 0x8000fbc0/0x8000fbc2)
+could reach the top 512 B, and two more reads of the table at 0x4028a7b8 indexed by a
+sum of two signed words >> 6 (MK2 0x4010d416 / 0x4010da60) could reach all of it.
+Whether any of them can go negative is UNKNOWN; they are reads, so what sits here could
+at worst be read by stock as a table value, and nothing here is written at run time.
+"""
+
+
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------- claims
+#
+# (The mods' claims, detours and patches are added with the MK2 Sample Focus builds.)
